{#
  Post-quantum MACsec. Two global leaves.

  /native/access-session/pqc-type
  /native/access-session/tls-version

  This is the payoff for all of macsec-eaptls. Everything that role built exists so
  that these two leaves have somewhere to apply: a TLS 1.3 handshake inside EAP-TLS,
  whose key exchange these steer.

  pqc-type is an enum with four values and the naming is actively misleading:

    pqc      pure mode. Lands on ML-KEM-512, the WEAKER post-quantum choice here.
    hybrid   x448 + ML-KEM-768. Strongest, and it fails closed: a peer that cannot do
             ML-KEM is refused rather than downgraded.
    non-pqc  classical secp521r1. Troubleshooting only.
    all      hybrid between two IOS XE peers, but silently drops to secp521r1 against
             a non-PQ peer and still reports "Secured".

  Default is non-pqc, so this is a leaf you have to set: an EAP-TLS session left alone
  is classical.

  tls-version pinned to 1.3 because ML-KEM in EAP-TLS only exists in the TLS 1.3
  handshake. The shipped default is "all", which means a peer can land on 1.2 and take
  the post-quantum key exchange off the table without anything looking wrong.

  On teardown these go back to the shipped defaults rather than being removed, so the
  EAP-TLS session keeps working and simply becomes classical again. Removing them would
  do the same thing, but saying it explicitly means the running config shows what you
  chose.
-#}
<config xmlns="urn:ietf:params:xml:ns:netconf:base:1.0"
        xmlns:nc="urn:ietf:params:xml:ns:netconf:base:1.0">
  <native xmlns="http://cisco.com/ns/yang/Cisco-IOS-XE-native">
    <access-session xmlns="http://cisco.com/ns/yang/Cisco-IOS-XE-sanet">
{% if state == 'present' %}
      <pqc-type>{{ pq_macsec_type }}</pqc-type>
      <tls-version>{{ pq_macsec_tls_version }}</tls-version>
{% else %}
      <pqc-type>non-pqc</pqc-type>
      <tls-version>all</tls-version>
{% endif %}
    </access-session>
  </native>
</config>
